from chemistry_helpers import TOPICS
from chemistry_qa_writer import q,write_bank
items=[
q(1,'Which ion makes an aqueous solution acidic?','H⁺.','4.4.2.4','2019 Q02.1'),
q(1,'Give the formula of the ion responsible for alkalinity.','OH⁻.','4.4.2.4','2023 Q05.4'),
q(1,'What type of salt is formed using nitric acid?','Nitrate.','4.4.2.2'),
q(1,'At which electrode does reduction take place during electrolysis?','Cathode.','4.4.3.5'),
q(1,'What is meant by a weak acid?','An acid that is only partially ionised in aqueous solution.','4.4.2.6','2023 Q05.1'),
q(1,'Which gas forms when magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid?','Hydrogen.','4.4.2.1'),
q(2,'Explain why copper does not react with dilute hydrochloric acid.','- Copper is **less reactive than hydrogen**.\n- It cannot **displace hydrogen** from this dilute acid.','4.4.1.2; 4.4.2.1'),
q(2,'Write a balanced equation for magnesium reacting with hydrochloric acid.','**Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂**. The two hydrogen atoms form one H₂ molecule.','4.4.2.1','2023 Q08.5'),
q(2,'Write the ionic equation for neutralisation, including state symbols.','**H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)**.','4.4.2.4'),
q(2,'Zinc is placed in copper sulfate solution. State two expected observations.','- A **brown copper deposit** forms.\n- The **blue colour fades** as copper ions leave the solution.','4.4.1.2','2022 Q04.5'),
q(2,'Explain why carbon can extract copper from copper oxide but cannot extract aluminium from aluminium oxide.','- Carbon is **more reactive than copper**, so it can remove oxygen from copper oxide.\n- Aluminium is **more reactive than carbon**, so its oxide cannot be reduced by carbon in this way.','4.4.1.3','2022 Q07.1–07.3; 2023 Q09.5'),
q(2,'Why is excess copper oxide added when preparing copper sulfate from sulfuric acid?','- It ensures **all the acid reacts**.\n- Any unreacted insoluble oxide can then be **removed by filtration**.','4.4.2.3','2022 Q04.1–04.2'),
q(2,'Name a suitable indicator for adding acid to sodium hydroxide in a titration, and state its endpoint colour change.','- Phenolphthalein.\n- **Pink to colourless**.','4.4.2.5','2023 Q05.3'),
q(2,'Why does solid sodium chloride not undergo electrolysis, whereas molten sodium chloride does?','- In the solid, ions are **fixed in a lattice**.\n- When molten, ions are **free to move to electrodes and carry charge**.','4.4.3.1','2022 Q06.3'),
q(2,'Write the half-equation for aluminium forming from aluminium ions. State whether this is oxidation or reduction.','- **Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al**.\n- **Reduction**, because electrons are gained.','4.4.3.3; 4.4.3.5','2019 Q07.2; 2023 Q07.1'),
q(2,'Explain why aluminium oxide is mixed with cryolite for electrolysis.','- The mixture has a **lower melting/operating temperature** than pure aluminium oxide.\n- This reduces the **energy needed for heating**.','4.4.3.3','2019 Q07.1'),
q(2,'How does hydrogen ion concentration change when pH changes from 5 to 2?','- It **increases**.\n- By **10³ = 1,000 times**, because each decrease of one pH unit gives a tenfold increase.','4.4.2.6','2023 Q05.6'),
q(3,'Metal A displaces B from its salt. Metal C displaces A. B does not displace A or C. Deduce the reactivity order and explain what greater reactivity means in terms of ions.','- Order: **C > A > B**.\n- A metal displaces a **less reactive** metal from its compound.\n- More reactive metals have a greater tendency to **lose electrons and form positive ions**.','4.4.1.2','2023 Q09.4'),
q(3,'Write the ionic equation for zinc displacing copper and identify which species is oxidised and which is reduced.','- **Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu**.\n- Zinc is **oxidised**, losing two electrons.\n- Copper ions are **reduced**, gaining two electrons.','4.4.1.4','2022 Q04.5–04.6; 2023 Q09.3'),
q(3,'Write a balanced equation for copper carbonate reacting with sulfuric acid, and name the gas test.','- **CuCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O + CO₂**.\n- Bubble the gas through **limewater**.\n- It becomes **cloudy**, identifying carbon dioxide.','4.4.2.2'),
q(3,'Explain why the carbon anodes must be replaced during aluminium extraction.','- **Oxygen forms at the positive electrode**.\n- It **reacts with the carbon**, forming carbon dioxide.\n- The carbon anodes are consumed and wear away.','4.4.3.3','2019 Q07.4'),
q(3,'Predict both products when molten lead bromide is electrolysed using inert electrodes. Write the anode half-equation.','- Lead forms at the **negative cathode**.\n- Bromine forms at the **positive anode**.\n- **2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻**.','4.4.3.2; 4.4.3.5'),
q(3,'Predict the products from aqueous sodium sulfate with inert electrodes and explain your choices.','- **Hydrogen at the cathode**: sodium is more reactive than hydrogen.\n- **Oxygen at the anode**: the solution contains no halide ions.\n- Water supplies hydrogen and hydroxide ions, which compete with the dissolved salt ions.','4.4.3.4','2022 Q06.4–06.6; 2023 Q07.2–07.3'),
q(3,'Why should a titration burette be rinsed with acid, but the conical flask may be rinsed with distilled water?','- Water left in the burette would **dilute the acid** and change its concentration.\n- Rinsing with acid avoids that dilution.\n- Water in the conical flask does **not change the moles of alkali**, so it does not change the reacting acid amount.','4.4.2.5'),
q(3,'A student obtains titres 24.60 cm³ (rough), 24.20 cm³, 24.10 cm³ and 24.15 cm³. Using a concordance tolerance of 0.10 cm³, calculate the mean titre and explain the selection.','- Exclude the **rough trial**.\n- The three accurate titres are within 0.10 cm³ of each other.\n- Mean = (24.20 + 24.10 + 24.15)/3 = **24.15 cm³**.','4.4.2.5','2022 Q08.2'),
q(4,'Compare a dilute strong acid and a concentrated weak acid. Explain why strength alone is insufficient to decide which sample has the lower pH.','- Strong means **fully ionised**; weak means **partly ionised** in water.\n- Dilute means little acid per volume; concentrated means much acid per volume.\n- pH depends on the resulting **H⁺ concentration**, which depends on both properties.\n- Without concentrations and ionisation information, strength alone cannot determine the lower pH.','4.4.2.6','2022 Q08.1'),
q(4,'Copper chloride solution is electrolysed in the apparatus below using inert electrodes. Name the products and write both electrode half-equations.\n\n![Electrolysis setup with a negative cathode and positive anode.](../Diagrams/electrolysis.svg)','- Cathode product: **copper**.\n- **Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu**.\n- Anode product: **chlorine**.\n- **2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻**.','4.4.3.4–4.4.3.5','2023 Q07.1–07.3'),
q(4,'Compare the products of molten and aqueous sodium chloride electrolysis, using inert electrodes. Explain the difference at the cathode.','- Molten: **sodium at cathode, chlorine at anode**.\n- Aqueous: **hydrogen at cathode, chlorine at anode**.\n- The aqueous solution also contains ions from **water**.\n- Sodium is more reactive than hydrogen, so hydrogen is produced instead of sodium in the aqueous solution.','4.4.3.2–4.4.3.4','2019 Q07.6; 2022 Q06.1–06.6'),
q(4,'Write the anode half-equation for oxygen production from hydroxide ions. Show that charge and atoms balance.','- **4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻**.\n- Both sides contain **four oxygen atoms and four hydrogen atoms**.\n- Left total charge is −4; right total charge is also −4 from the four electrons.\n- Electrons are lost, so this is **oxidation**.','4.4.3.5','2023 Q07.3'),
q(5,'25.0 cm³ NaOH is neutralised by 18.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ H₂SO₄. Calculate NaOH concentration in mol/dm³ and g/dm³. H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O. Mᵣ: NaOH = 40.','- Acid volume = **0.0180 dm³**.\n- Acid moles = 0.100 × 0.0180 = **0.00180 mol**.\n- NaOH moles = 2 × 0.00180 = **0.00360 mol**.\n- NaOH concentration = 0.00360/0.0250 = **0.144 mol/dm³**.\n- In g/dm³: 0.144 × 40 = **5.76 g/dm³**.','4.4.2.5','2019 Q09.5; 2022 Q08.3; 2023 Q05.5'),
q(6,'Describe how to prepare a pure, dry sample of copper sulfate crystals using copper oxide and dilute sulfuric acid. Explain the main stages.','- Wear goggles and **gently warm dilute sulfuric acid**, then remove the burner.\n- Add copper oxide in small portions while stirring until **excess solid remains**, ensuring all acid has reacted.\n- **Filter** to remove the excess insoluble oxide; keep the copper sulfate filtrate.\n- Use a water bath or electric heater to **evaporate some water**, concentrating the solution without strongly heating it to dryness.\n- Leave to **cool and crystallise**.\n- Filter crystals, rinse with a little cold distilled water and dry between filter papers.','4.4.2.3','2022 Q04.1–04.4; 2023 Q03.1'),
q(6,'Describe how to find accurate reacting volumes for hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide by titration.','- Rinse a volumetric pipette with alkali and use a filler to transfer a fixed volume to a conical flask. Add a few drops of phenolphthalein; use a white tile and goggles.\n- Rinse the burette with acid, fill it, remove the funnel and ensure the jet has no air bubble.\n- Record the **initial meniscus reading at eye level**.\n- Add acid while swirling; add **drop by drop near the endpoint** until the first permanent pink-to-colourless change.\n- Record the final reading; **titre = final − initial**.\n- After a rough trial, repeat to obtain **concordant accurate titres** and calculate their mean, excluding the rough trial.','4.4.2.5','2019 Q02.5'),
q(6,'Plan an investigation to test whether changing an aqueous salt changes electrolysis products. Include control variables and product identification.','- Predict products for two solutions, such as copper chloride and sodium sulfate, using the ions present and reactivity rules.\n- Use a low-voltage DC supply and **inert electrodes**, keeping them apart. Identify each electrode polarity.\n- Keep concentration, volume, voltage, electrode area/separation and reaction time constant. Use clean electrodes to prevent contamination.\n- Observe each electrode separately and collect any gases: hydrogen gives a pop; oxygen relights a glowing splint; chlorine bleaches damp litmus. Look for a copper deposit as well.\n- Repeat each trial and compare observations with predictions; check the circuit before treating lack of bubbles as evidence of no product.\n- Wear goggles, use the instructed ventilation for chlorine and switch off before adjusting equipment.','4.4.3.4')]
write_bank(TOPICS[3],'Chemical changes',items,'- Salts and preparation: 2022 Q04.1–04.4; 2023 Q03.1.\n- Titrations: 2019 Q02.5 and Q09.5; 2022 Q08.2–08.3; 2023 Q05.3–05.5.\n- Electrolysis: 2019 Q07.1–07.6; 2022 Q06.1–06.6; 2023 Q07.1–07.5.\n- Extraction/redox: 2022 Q04.5–04.6 and Q07.1–07.3; 2023 Q09.1–09.5.\n- Strong/weak acids: 2022 Q08.1; 2023 Q05.1.\n\nThese broad themes recur in the sample. Other prompts provide specification-based coverage. The questions, values and allocations here are original.')
